//
// `debug_assert` is a reserved name, and using it says so.
//
// @PLN53 A2.3 adds `debug_assert(test, message)` as the `assert` companion that `--release`
// elides, and the word is reserved ahead of that build so user code cannot take it
// (`898bcc3a`, which states the contract this file guards: *"using it as a function name now
// produces a clear parse error"*).  It did not.  Every other keyword either has a parser arm
// or is defined in the default library, so a statement starting with `debug_assert` fell
// through the whole dispatch chain and was reported as a missing `;` — at the PREVIOUS
// statement's line, naming neither the word nor the reason (loft#1167).
//
// The refusal lives in TWO places because a keyword token reaches neither an identifier
// lookup nor a call: the statement chain in `parser/expressions.rs` and the value-expression
// entry `parse_single` in `parser/vectors.rs`.  Either alone leaves the other position on the
// old confusing message.
//
// ⚠ SCOPE, measured: `debug_assert = 5` — the word as an ASSIGNMENT TARGET — still reports
// `Expect token ;` at the `=`.  That is not special to this word and is not a regression:
// every reserved word answers an assignment target from its own arm (`return` says "Expect no
// expression after return", `match` "Expect token {", `sizeof` "Expect token (").  Using a
// keyword as a variable name is a different question from calling a reserved builtin, and the
// line is correct in all four.
//
// This file is `@EXPECT_ERROR`, so it stops at the first refusal; the call-in-value-position
// shape below it is covered by the same arm and is exercised by the probe in loft#1167.
//
// @falsified-at: 419fd056 — on the MESSAGE channel, which `make falsify` does not read.
// That tool compares exit / asserts / leak / panic, and both builds REFUSE this program, so
// it reports INERT and that is the correct reading rather than a broken guard: the control
// exits 1 saying `Expect token ;` and this tree exits 1 saying `debug_assert` is reserved.
// The discriminator is the text, and it is the `@EXPECT_ERROR` line above that reads it —
// measured directly: the control's output contains "reserved for a future release" 0 times,
// so the harness's expectation fails on it.
//   ARMED: none — this sweep ran without `LOFT_STRICT_STORES`, and the channel above moved
//   anyway, so the instrument is not needed to see it.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.

// @EXPECT_ERROR: `debug_assert` is reserved for a future release
fn main() {
  da_x = 1;
  debug_assert(da_x == 1, "the reserved word must refuse, not fall through");
  println("unreachable — the compile must fail above");
}
